// stdlib/flame/forge_det.hexa — forge determinism API
// #4214 canonical upgrade: torch.use_deterministic_algorithms() reference-match.
//
// PRIMARY surface (1st-class, torch ref-match):
//   set_deterministic(true/false)  — in-process call, overrides env
//   is_deterministic() -> bool     — query effective det mode
//
// ESCAPE-HATCH (shell/CI/legacy, CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG analogue):
//   HEXA_DET=1 env var — read when API was never called in-process
//
// Precedence: API (hexa_forge_set_deterministic) > env (HEXA_DET).
// Default (no API call + env unset) = fast non-det (training default).
//
// Eval/verdict/decode safety-pin: those entry points call
// set_deterministic(true) directly instead of exporting HEXA_DET,
// solving the in-process eval caveat (hexa setenv absence irrelevant).
//
// torch ref: torch.use_deterministic_algorithms(True/False)
//   torch source: torch/csrc/autograd/python_variable.cpp + aten/src/ATen/Context.cpp
//   torch env fallback: CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG (low-level knob, not 1st-class)

// NATIVE BUILTINS (no import required) — `set_deterministic`/`is_deterministic`
// are now first-class runtime builtins, registered like `cuda_available`
// (carrier + HexaVal-ABI impl in self/runtime_emit_full.hexa, bridging the C-ABI
// hexa_forge_set_deterministic in runtime_cuda.c / runtime.h). Call them directly
// anywhere — no `import "stdlib/flame/forge_det.hexa"` needed:
//
//   set_deterministic(true)    // det mode ON  (fixed-order non-atomic · byte-exact)
//   set_deterministic(false)   // det mode OFF (fast atomic kernels · training default)
//   is_deterministic()         // -> int (1 if det active via API or HEXA_DET env)
//
// WHY BUILTINS, not an `extern fn` here: declaring `extern fn
// hexa_forge_set_deterministic` made the hexat emit an FFI dlsym wrapper named
// `hexa_forge_set_deterministic` (HexaVal ABI), which collided with runtime.h's
// C-ABI `void hexa_forge_set_deterministic(int)` → clang "conflicting types"
// (the canonical surface never compiled). The carrier/builtin path avoids the
// name collision entirely. This file is now documentation only.
